  "source_text": "The source was referenced only by a codename: CARDINAL.\n\nPer Cardinal — Venn cloud segmentation updated Q3. New DMZ architecture attached. Credential rotation schedule shifted to 14-day cycle. Adjust maps accordingly.\n\nCardinal confirms: Barrington media servers migrated to Frankfurt data center. Internal routing unchanged. Access points via legacy VPN still viable.\n\nReceived from Cardinal: Cole HQ security audit results. Network penetration test identified 3 unpatched vulnerabilities in building management system. Cross-reference with Courier's physical access plan.\n\nAdrian sat back. The bread smell from the bakery below seemed suddenly inappropriate, like laughing at a funeral.\n\nCARDINAL. Someone above Seb. Someone with direct, current intelligence about the internal systems of every target company. Someone who could provide administrative credentials, security audit results, network architecture updates — information that required either an extraordinarily sophisticated hacking operation or a seat at the table where these things were discussed.\n\nAdrian's investor brain, the part of him that had spent years evaluating tech companies, understanding their structures, assessing their vulnerabilities, began connecting dots. During his years as an angel investor and advisor, he'd sat in board meetings, reviewed security audits, and had access to technical briefings that executives received about their own infrastructure. He knew what these documents looked like. He knew what level of access they implied.\n\nThis wasn't hacked data. This was delivered data. Someone with legitimate access to these companies' most sensitive internal information had been feeding it to the movement through layers of separation.\n\nWho had access to the internal security architecture of six different oligarch companies simultaneously?\n\nThe question sent a chill through him that had nothing to do with the Swiss winter.\n\nHe pulled up Priya's files on Kai's platform, the content company, the short-form video empire. The maps were detailed. Comprehensive. And in the margins of her working notes: Cardinal confirms algorithm core accessible via dev-ops bridge. Do not attempt direct access — monitoring is anomaly-based, not signature-based. Cardinal will provide timing window.\n\nCardinal would provide a timing window. Into Kai's own company.\n\nAdrian stared at the screen. His mind constructed and discarded hypotheses. A mole inside each company? Possible but implausible; the odds of placing insiders in six separate organizations without detection were astronomical. A state intelligence agency? They'd have the capability but not the motivation; no government would want this data public. A rival oligarch, playing the others against each other?\n\nThat last thought lingered. A rival oligarch. Someone on the council itself, feeding intelligence to the movement while sitting at the table.\n\nLaine. It had to be Laine.\n\nThe Philanthropist's occasional moral hesitations in council meetings. His foundation's global reach, which could provide cover for intelligence gathering across multiple companies. His stated commitment to \"doing good,\" what if that commitment was more radical than anyone suspected? What if the man who publicly fretted about \"going too far\" was privately going much further?\n\nIt fit. Not perfectly, nothing in this world fit perfectly, but well enough to be plausible. And in a landscape of impossible things, plausibility was the best you could ask for.\n\nAdrian made a note in his own encrypted files: CARDINAL — likely council member. Laine? Investigate further. Do not raise with team until confirmed.\n\nHe turned back to the maps. Whatever Cardinal's identity, the intelligence was good, better than good, it was surgical. And with Priya gone, Adrian was the only person who could use it to complete the network architecture that Project Lumen needed.\n\nHe opened the files on Venn's cloud infrastructure, the Machine's systems, the digital backbone that half the world's computing ran on, and began checking Priya's completed routes against the latest updates.\n\nHis years as an investor had given him something Priya hadn't possessed: an understanding of how these companies thought about their own technology. Not just the architecture, but the philosophy. The assumptions built into the design. The blind spots created by corporate culture. Venn's systems, for example, were optimized for reliability: uptime, redundancy, failover. Everything was designed to keep the lights on. The monitoring systems were focused outward, detecting external threats, preventing service interruptions. Internal threats, the possibility that someone inside the network was exfiltrating data, were a secondary concern, because Venn's corporate culture assumed that insiders were trustworthy.",
